WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
Injection happens when untrusted input is interpreted as instructions in the wrong context.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.040
For Java developers, that context can be much broader than SQL.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:25.900
Sensitive sinks include database queries, ORM queries, LDAP filters, XPath and XML processing, shell commands, templates, expression languages, browser output, file paths, URLs, message systems, and downstream APIs.

4
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.200
The consistent task is recognizing when data is about to cross into something that interprets it.

5
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.120
Strong prevention combines several controls.

6
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.220
Map data flow from trust boundaries to sensitive sinks.

7
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.200
Validate input for business intent and shape.

8
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.940
Use parameterized APIs for query construction.

9
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.040
Prefer Java libraries over shell commands where practical.

10
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.540
Configure parsers defensively.

11
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.840
Use context-specific output encoding for web responses.

12
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.380
Apply least privilege so mistakes have less impact.

13
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:04.120
Review dependencies that interpret strings, evaluate expressions, or accept flexible query objects.

14
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.720
Do not rely on one universal fix.

15
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.700
Escaping is not the same as parameterization.

16
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.840
Sanitization is not the same as output encoding.

17
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.300
Client-side validation is not a server-side boundary.

18
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.080
ORMs do not automatically make every dynamic query safe.

19
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.580
Sandboxes do not justify evaluating untrusted expressions.

20
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.400
Each interpreter has its own rules, so the control must fit the sink.

21
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.760
The professional habit is designing Java applications where untrusted data cannot change command meaning.

22
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:45.460
That means using types, allowlists, safe APIs, narrow interfaces, focused tests, and review practices that make risky transitions obvious.

23
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:55.160
When teams build these patterns into ordinary development, injection prevention stops being a last-minute audit exercise and becomes part of clean application design.
